Reason:
<text>

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (enable) {
    pm_runtime_get_sync(ch->dev);
    ret = rz_mtu3_initialize_counter(counter, count->id);
    ...
} else {
    rz_mtu3_terminate_counter(counter, count->id);
    pm_runtime_put(ch->dev);
}

These statements are executed unconditionally each time the enable sysfs file is written, without checking whether the requested enable state matches the current state. This causes multiple increments/decrements of the runtime PM usage count (leading to underflow or imbalance) and redundant hardware operations (e.g., terminating an already disabled channel with clocks off).

The code statements that fix the bug are:
+ mutex_lock(&priv->lock);
+ if (priv->count_is_enabled[count->id] == enable)
+     goto exit;
...
+exit:
+ mutex_unlock(&priv->lock);

The fix adds a check against the cached `count_is_enabled` value before performing any runtime PM or hardware operations. If the user writes the same enable value, the function exits early (after acquiring and releasing the lock), preventing unbalanced PM usage count and unnecessary hardware access. The mutex is moved to protect the entire critical section, including the read of the cached state.

